WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.880
I'm back with Reiner Pope, CEO&nbsp;
of MatX, a new AI chip company.&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.440
Last time we were talking about&nbsp;
what happens inside a data center.&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.560
Now I want to understand what&nbsp;
happens inside an AI chip.&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.680
How does a chip actually work?
Full disclosure, by the way:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.440
I am an angel investor in MatX.
So hopefully you have designed a good chip.&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.840
Hope so. I'll start with the smallest&nbsp;
fundamental unit of chip design, and we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.880
build up to what an actual production&nbsp;
chip is and what its components are.&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.480
At the very bottom level of a chip, the&nbsp;
primitives we work with are logic gates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.640
very simple things like AND, OR, and NOT.
These are connected together by wires that have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:50.080
be laid out physically as metal traces on a chip.
The main function that AI chips want to compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.720
is the multiplication of matrices.
Inside that, the fundamental primitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.680
is a multiply-accumulate of pairs of numbers.
We're going to demonstrate what that calculation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.280
looks like by hand, and then infer what&nbsp;
a circuit would look like for that.&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:17.360
It'll be easiest if I do a multiply-accumulate of&nbsp;
a four-bit number with another four-bit number.&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.080
The clearest primitive is&nbsp;
actually multiply-accumulate.&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:40.000
So there's a multiply of these two terms, and&nbsp;
then we're going to add in an eight-bit number.&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:47.840
Can I ask a clarifying question?
Why is this the natural primitive for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.600
whatever computation happens inside a computer?
There are a few reasons.&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.960
It's a little bit more efficient, but the&nbsp;
reason it's natural for AI chips is that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.400
you look at what's happening during a matrix&nbsp;
multiply… What is a matrix multiply in short?&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:21.520
There's a for-loop over i, over&nbsp;
j, and over k, of output [i,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:36.080
k] += input [i, j] x other input [j, k].
A multiply-accumulate happens at every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.640
single step of a matrix multiply.
The other observation is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.520
precision will almost always be higher in the&nbsp;
accumulation step than in the multiplication step.&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.840
This is specific to AI chips.
You're multiplying low-precision numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.240
and then when you accumulate, errors accumulate&nbsp;
quickly, so you need more precision there.&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.240
This is why we've chosen to do a four-bit&nbsp;
multiplication and an eight-bit addition.&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.440
Let me make sure I understood that.
There are two ways to understand that.&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.000
One is that the value will&nbsp;
be larger than the inputs.&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.800
The other is that if it was a&nbsp;
floating-point number it would be…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.160
Maybe that part is less intuitive to me.
But maybe it's the same principle?&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.400
It really is the same principle.
The separate principle is that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.680
you're summing up this number, you're summing&nbsp;
up a whole bunch of numbers, so you've got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.000
lot of rounding errors accumulating.
Whereas in this case, there's only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.560
one multiplication in the chain,&nbsp;
so there aren't a lot of rounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.920
errors accumulating in the multiplication.
Why are you summing up a whole bunch of numbers?&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.280
There’s just two numbers there.
This summation is repeated j many times.&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.480
Any errors accumulate. I see.
So how would we perform this calculation by hand?&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.240
As a human, we would probably&nbsp;
separate it into two, but we can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:55.760
it all in one using long multiplication.
For the multiplication term first, we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:03.120
going to multiply this four-bit number by every&nbsp;
single bit position in the other four-bit number.&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.720
We write that out. First, 1001&nbsp;
multiplied by this bit position.&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.800
That is the number itself.
Then shifted across by one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.720
we're multiplying by 0.
That gives us an all-0 number.&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:28.800
Shifted across one more to&nbsp;
multiply by this one, we get 1001.&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.440
Finally, for this last bit position,&nbsp;
we get an all-0 number again.&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.960
This gives us a bunch of terms that&nbsp;
we have to add for the multiplication.&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:50.160
While we're doing that summation, we might as&nbsp;
well add in the actual accumulator term as well.&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:05:00.000
So we just copy that directly across.
So this is the sum.&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.720
It's a five-way sum that we want to compute.&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.160
What logic gates did it take us&nbsp;
to get to this intermediate step?&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.960
We needed to produce all 16&nbsp;
of these partial products.&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:23.920
How do I produce one of these partial products?
Let's take this number 1, for example here.&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.720
We produce it by multiplying this&nbsp;
number by this one over here.&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.600
We can produce that with an AND gate.
This number is 1 if both this bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.920
is 1 and this bit is 1.
If either of them is 0,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:45.600
then the multiplication of 0 times anything is 0.
To produce all of this, we ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.880
consuming 16 AND gates.
In the general case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:06:06.560
if I were doing a p bit multiply times a q bit&nbsp;
multiply, this will be p times q many ANDs.&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:15.040
Finally, I sum them. Most of the work&nbsp;
is going to happen in the summing.&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.480
Let me describe the other&nbsp;
logic gate that we use here.&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.200
AND is almost the simplest logic&nbsp;
gate that exists on a chip.&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:30.480
It's almost the smallest. At the other extreme,&nbsp;
the very largest logic gate you'll typically use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:39.040
is something called a full adder.
Coming from software, you might think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:43.680
that a full adder adds 32-bit numbers together.
In this case, it just adds three single-bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.320
numbers together, so you can think&nbsp;
of it as adding 0, 1, and 1 together.&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.320
When I add these together, the result can&nbsp;
be 0, 1, 2, or 3, so I can express that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:58.880
in binary using just two bits.
As input, it has three bits.&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.960
As output, it has two bits.
The number 2 in binary is 10.&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:17.600
This is also known as a 3→2 compressor&nbsp;
because it takes three bits of input&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.520
and produces two bits of output.
Just to make sure I understood: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:25.200
two inputs are an X and a Y value&nbsp;
and then some carry that came in…&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:34.320
The three inputs are all bits in the same bit&nbsp;
position, like three bits in a column here.&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:41.360
The two outputs, I've drawn them vertically&nbsp;
here and horizontally here to match this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.920
vertical versus horizontal layout.
This expresses that things in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.160
column are in the same bit position, whereas&nbsp;
things in adjacent columns are different.&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:56.800
This is a carry out, whereas this was the sum.
So if the inputs in the full adder were, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.280
101, then the output would be 10.
If it were 111, it'd be 11.&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:06.800
If it were 000, it'd be 00.
If it were 010, it'd still be 01.&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.440
Got it.
Yeah. It's essentially just counting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:17.120
number of things and expressing that in binary.
This circuit captures what we as humans naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:24.960
do when we're summing along a column.
I'll show one iteration of using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:27.840
the full adder to sum.
The way I sum here is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.760
to be a little unnatural for humans.
We would sum along the column and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:38.160
remember the carry, but instead of remembering&nbsp;
the carry, we'll explicitly write it out.&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.160
We proceed from the rightmost&nbsp;
column toward the left.&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:51.360
On the rightmost column, we sum the 1 and the 1,&nbsp;
and that produces a zero here and a carry of one.&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:59.200
We've used this full adder circuit on this pair&nbsp;
of bits and produced a pair of bits as output.&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.880
Now we can do the same thing with this column.
We have a column of four numbers, so we'll take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:11.520
the first three of them, run a full adder on&nbsp;
them, and that gives us a 0 and a 0 as output.&nbsp;

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:18.720
The sum of these is 00.
That's the full adder applied to all these bits.&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:25.840
As I've used up bits, I'll cross them&nbsp;
out to indicate that I've handled them.&nbsp;

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:31.200
Let's keep going a little bit more.
I take these three numbers, I add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.880
them, and that gives me a 1 and a 0.
I've dealt with these three numbers.&nbsp;

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:44.160
Now I take these three numbers and add&nbsp;
them, and that gives me a 1 and a 0,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:51.840
and I've dealt with these numbers.
The way to view this is that I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:57.120
this whole grid of numbers that need to be added.
I'm going to keep applying full adders to all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:03.600
bits here, constantly removing three numbers from&nbsp;
a column and writing out two numbers as output.&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:09.200
Keep going over and over again until I&nbsp;
eventually get just one single number coming out.&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:30.800
This approach is called a Dadda multiplier.
This is the standard for how you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:38.400
area-efficient multipliers using full adders.
Let's try to quantify the circuit size of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.440
this so we have a sense of how big&nbsp;
things are and can compare them later.&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:49.360
How many full adders did I use?
How many numbers did I start with?&nbsp;

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:57.200
I have the 16 partial products, which is a product&nbsp;
of all of these terms with all of these terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:06.480
plus the eight terms that I'm adding here.
I started off with 24 bits.&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:14.480
Eventually I produced eight bits on the output.
In every step, I was crossing off three numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:20.080
and writing two numbers out as a result.
Every single use of a full adder eliminates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:23.840
one of the bits here.
So how many full adders?&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.800
It must be 24 minus 8, so there&nbsp;
were 16 full adders in this circuit.&nbsp;

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.000
This is true in the general case as well.
There will be p times q many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:38.400
full adders in this circuit.
Let me make sure I understand the logic of that.&nbsp;

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:42.407
The input bits, 24, is p x q, plus p + q.
The output bits are just p + q.&nbsp;

00:11:42.407 --> 00:12:00.080
So p x q plus p + q, minus p + q equals p x q.
That's right. I think this explains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:06.400
or at least hints at, the second reason&nbsp;
we chose to do a multiply-accumulate.&nbsp;

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.960
The first reason is that it's what&nbsp;
shows up in matrix multiplication.&nbsp;

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:16.080
The second is that it gave us this very&nbsp;
slick, simple p x q, very simple algebra.&nbsp;

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:22.480
We've described this whole procedure.
Every single atomic step that I took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.640
here becomes a logic gate, and then&nbsp;
the wires are connected together.&nbsp;

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:31.840
When I had these three inputs that I used to&nbsp;
produce these two outputs, if I think of mapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:38.560
this to a physical device, there would be a wire&nbsp;
connecting all three of these things together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:46.720
into a logic gate that produced this output.
This is the main primitive, at different bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:54.000
widths, that's inside an AI chip.
We're going to build up from here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:58.000
to how you would use it to run all&nbsp;
the other operations you might want.&nbsp;

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:02.800
This might be the wrong time to ask, but&nbsp;
whenever Nvidia reports that this chip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:11.120
can do X many FP4 or half as many FP8, it&nbsp;
seems to imply those circuits are fungible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:17.760
that there's not a dedicated FP4 versus FP8.
But the way you're mapping it out here, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:27.440
like if it has to be mapped out in the logic, you&nbsp;
would need a dedicated FP4 multiply-accumulate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:33.040
then a dedicated FP8 accumulate.
Can you "funge" them?&nbsp;

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:37.920
As drawn, they're not particularly fungible.
This is actually one of the main choices you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:42.560
have to make when designing a chip: how&nbsp;
much of FP4 and how much of FP8 do I have?&nbsp;

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:47.760
Sometimes I'll make that consideration from&nbsp;
the point of view of the customer requirement.&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:55.280
Another angle is to equalize the&nbsp;
power budget between FP4 and FP8.&nbsp;

00:13:55.280 --> 00:14:02.000
When they report those numbers and it just happens&nbsp;
to be the case that it does 2x as many FP4 as FP8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:08.320
they're just choosing to give equivalent&nbsp;
die areas to all the floating points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:12.160
and as a result it ends up being…?
Why is the ratio exactly 2x?&nbsp;

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:18.480
Part of it is that surely it won't&nbsp;
be exactly equivalent to die area.&nbsp;

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:23.520
There's a data movement reason.
We'll maybe come back to this when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:28.800
look at how it goes into and out of memories.
There's something really nice from a software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:32.080
level about the fact that I can pack&nbsp;
two four-bit numbers into the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:34.640
storage as an eight-bit number.
When I store that to memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:44.960
the sizing of the buses that I wire out within&nbsp;
the chip makes that work out really nicely.&nbsp;

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:52.160
Come to think of it, it's not just 2x.
The amount of area it takes sounds like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.160 --> 00:15:00.240
it's quadratic with the bit length.
That's why smaller precision is even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.960
more favorable than you might think.
This is a really big reason.&nbsp;

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:10.640
In fact, Nvidia made a change.
Historically, up until B100 or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:16.400
B200, every time you halved the bit&nbsp;
precision, you doubled the FLOP count.&nbsp;

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:21.680
For the reason you said, because of this quadratic&nbsp;
scaling, that ratio is actually slightly wrong.&nbsp;

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:26.480
You should get an even bigger speedup&nbsp;
than you might otherwise think.&nbsp;

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:34.960
Nvidia’s product specs have started acknowledging&nbsp;
that in B300 and beyond, where the FP4 is three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:38.560
times faster than the FP8.
Though it should be 4x.&nbsp;

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.160
Yeah. What I've shown here is the&nbsp;
simplest case of integer multiply.&nbsp;

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:48.480
When you're dealing with floating point, as&nbsp;
you do in FP4 and FP8, there's this other term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:54.880
the exponent, that complicates the calculation.
What can we see already from this?&nbsp;

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:58.560
I think the big observation you've made is that&nbsp;
there's this quadratic scaling with bit width,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:04.640
which is very effective and is&nbsp;
the single reason low-precision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:11.280
arithmetic has worked so well for neural nets.
The other thing we're going to do now is compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:17.360
the area spent on the multiplication&nbsp;
itself with all the circuitry around it.&nbsp;

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:27.760
We'll walk back in time a little bit and&nbsp;
see how GPUs prior to Tensor Cores worked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:34.560
which is in fact the same way CPUs worked.
Where do we stick this multiply-accumulate unit?&nbsp;

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:47.840
Generically, I'll describe a CUDA core or a CPU.
You'll have some register file which stores some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:58.480
number of entries, maybe eight entries of, in this&nbsp;
case, 4-bit numbers, but typically 32-bit numbers.&nbsp;

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:06.800
Inside the CUDA core, I'll have&nbsp;
some register file of some depth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:18.000
and then I'll have my multiply-accumulate circuit.
What it's going to do is take three arbitrary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:21.280
registers from this register file,&nbsp;
perform the multiply-accumulate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:25.920
and then write back to the register file.
It's going to write to this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:31.440
but it was able to read from this&nbsp;
one, this one, and another random one.&nbsp;

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:39.920
It will take three inputs like this.
This is the core data path of many processors.&nbsp;

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:42.640
Most processors look like this.
You've got some set of registers, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:50.800
then you've got some set of logic units, or ALUs.
We want to analyze the cost of the data movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:58.320
from the register file to the ALU and back.
Ultimately, there's going to be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:01.040
circuit that says, "Well, I don't&nbsp;
always have to select this guy.&nbsp;

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:04.480
I might select any of the&nbsp;
registers at any point in time."&nbsp;

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:11.920
The first question is: how can I build a circuit?
The circuit I'm going to look for is a mux.&nbsp;

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:17.680
In this case, it's going to have eight inputs,&nbsp;
one from each entry of the register file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:25.120
and it's going to have one output,&nbsp;
which is actually producing this output.&nbsp;

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:32.960
What is the cost of this thing?
All we have to build it out of is AND and OR.&nbsp;

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:37.680
How do we build it?
We do the dumbest thing possible.&nbsp;

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:44.640
We form a mask. When we want to read the&nbsp;
third entry, we're going to AND every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:48.000
single entry with either 1 or 0 based&nbsp;
on whether that's what we want to read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:52.240
and then we're going to OR all of them together.
Just to make sure I understand the basics.&nbsp;

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:59.360
What the mux is doing is just selecting an input?
Just selecting, invisible to software.&nbsp;

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.240
You say "I want input number three,"&nbsp;
and that means there's a mux here.&nbsp;

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:06.080
So what is the cost of this mux?
An n-input&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:21.120
mux operating on p bits.
I have n rows. That's just eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:25.600
rows, and each row is p bits wide.
I have to AND every single bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:32.320
so I get n x p many AND gates.
For every single input I have to decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:37.680
whether I'm going to mask it out or not.
Then I'm going to OR them all together.&nbsp;

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:47.040
There's going to be n – 1 times p many OR gates.
I've got all of these different things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:53.600
almost all of them are 0s, but I need to collapse&nbsp;
them from my eight options down into one option.&nbsp;

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:58.560
Every step, I need to OR one&nbsp;
row into an existing row.&nbsp;

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:04.240
It's actually funny that you don't&nbsp;
think at the level of hardware.&nbsp;

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:09.760
You just think, "Oh, I'll just select element&nbsp;
three," and something as simple as that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:15.280
in and of itself quite a complicated circuit.
This is the first step of all of the hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:22.480
data movement costs that show up.
We're just going to compare.&nbsp;

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:26.080
I have to pay this cost.
I've got one mux here, and in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:30.960
I have two more copies of that for each of the&nbsp;
three inputs to my multiply-accumulate operation.&nbsp;

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:42.640
I have this cost, which is 3 x n x p AND gates&nbsp;
over here, compared to p x q gates in the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:48.640
circuit that is doing the thing I care about.
If we plug in actual numbers, with n being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.760 --> 00:21:00.560
eight, I get 24 x p gates just in the data&nbsp;
movement, compared to—if q is four—4 x p&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:05.520
gates just in the multiply-adder.
Where is the three coming from?&nbsp;

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:14.720
Three different inputs here. What I'm&nbsp;
hinting at is that all of this work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:18.640
which scales as the size of the register&nbsp;
file—and this is a very small register&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:26.080
file—all of this work just moving the data from&nbsp;
the register file to the logic unit is many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:31.040
many times more expensive than the logic unit.
In the most recent ClusterMAX report, SemiAnalysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:36.480
ranked almost 100 different GPU clouds. Crusoe&nbsp;
was one of only five that made the gold tier.&nbsp;

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:41.120
SemiAnalysis found that gold-tier providers&nbsp;
like Crusoe had a total cost of ownership that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:46.160
was 5% to 15% lower than silver-tier ones,&nbsp;
even when they had identical GPU pricing.&nbsp;

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:50.480
This makes sense because total cost of ownership&nbsp;
is downstream of a bunch of different things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:54.480
don't necessarily show up in the sticker&nbsp;
price, but that Crusoe has optimized.&nbsp;

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:58.400
Things like how well you detect faults&nbsp;
and how quickly you replace failed nodes.&nbsp;

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:04.080
For example, Crusoe was one of the first clouds&nbsp;
to adopt NVSentinel, NVIDIA's own GPU monitoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:09.440
and self-healing software for enhanced&nbsp;
GPU uptime, utilization, and reliability.&nbsp;

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:14.240
This lets Crusoe make use of everything that&nbsp;
NVIDIA has learned about why chips fail across all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:18.880
their different fleets and deployments, so that&nbsp;
Crusoe can catch faults earlier in the process.&nbsp;

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:23.760
And once they identify a failure, Crusoe can&nbsp;
swap in a healthy node in less than 10 minutes.&nbsp;

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:27.200
Because they’re not running bare metal, Crusoe&nbsp;
doesn't have to spend time installing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:31.120
operating system or configuring drivers.
They can just spin up a new VM on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:34.800
already running and pre-qualified host.
If you want to learn more about this or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:41.840
the other reasons that Crusoe made&nbsp;
gold tier, go to crusoe.ai/dwarkesh.&nbsp;

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:44.480
It may be helpful to just&nbsp;
see what a mux looks like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:53.600
maybe a two-bit or a four-bit mux.
We’ll do a two-way mux.&nbsp;

00:22:53.600 --> 00:23:00.560
We've got two different numbers,&nbsp;
we’ve got these two inputs.&nbsp;

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:16.240
These are the inputs that are being selected&nbsp;
between, and the selector can either be "I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:24.080
this one" or "I want the other one."
This is a one-hot encoding.&nbsp;

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:34.160
This is what we start with.
Let's focus on this case.&nbsp;

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:40.480
This is the actual input we got, and we&nbsp;
want to produce this guy as the result.&nbsp;

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:47.840
Very laboriously, we AND&nbsp;
this bit with all of these.&nbsp;

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:55.200
That produces ANDing this bit with this row.
Likewise, we AND this bit with this row.&nbsp;

00:23:55.200 --> 00:24:07.360
That produces all 0s. There are four ANDs here.
Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:09.440
we OR these two together, and this gives a 1.
We OR these two together, this gives a 1.&nbsp;

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:15.120
We OR these two together, it gives a 0.
We OR these two together and it gives a 1.&nbsp;

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:22.560
Those are the four ORs.
This actually ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:27.280
looking a little bit like addition.
We did exactly the same set of ANDs.&nbsp;

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:34.080
We've ANDed all of these things together,&nbsp;
but then instead of collapsing it by using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:38.560
full adder circuits, we just get a&nbsp;
very simple collapsing with OR gates.&nbsp;

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:46.480
But that doesn't look like n times p.
This was with n=2 inputs.&nbsp;

00:24:48.320 --> 00:25:03.920
In the general case, we will have n&nbsp;
rows, and we'll have p bits per row.&nbsp;

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:11.680
That gives us the n times p many AND gates.
In this circuit I've described, almost all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:19.440
the cost, seven-eighths of the cost, is in reading&nbsp;
and writing the register file, and only a tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:23.280
fraction of the cost is in the logic unit itself.
This is the problem to solve.&nbsp;

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:31.760
This essentially was the state of play prior&nbsp;
to the Volta generation of Nvidia GPUs.&nbsp;

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:36.720
This kind of thing is what&nbsp;
was inside the CUDA cores.&nbsp;

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:41.760
This problem statement is what motivated&nbsp;
the introduction of Tensor Cores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:46.240
which are more generically called systolic arrays.
Think about how we're going to solve this problem.&nbsp;

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:48.480
We're spending almost all of our&nbsp;
circuit area on something that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:52.080
really don't care about and is hidden to the&nbsp;
software programmer, and the thing that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:57.520
actually care about is not much of the area.
Make this one bigger somehow while keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.520 --> 00:25:59.440
this at the same size.
That's the goal.&nbsp;

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:06.320
The evolution was that we had baked&nbsp;
this much into hardware at this stage.&nbsp;

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:11.280
This single line is a multiply-accumulate,&nbsp;
and this single thing was baked into hardware.&nbsp;

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:17.920
The idea of a systolic array is to go&nbsp;
two levels of loops up and bake this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:22.480
entire loop out here into hardware.
The idea is that if we have a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:27.280
bigger granularity fixed-function&nbsp;
piece of logic, maybe the taxes we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:30.400
pay on the input and output are much smaller.
Interesting. It sounds like you're suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:41.520
that if you go up one step in the matrix&nbsp;
multiply loop, you can tilt the balance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:46.080
more towards compute than communication.
That's right. There are two effects we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:49.280
going to take advantage of here.
One is that we can do more stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:58.640
per every trip through our register file.
The other is that in some of this loop, we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:07.040
take advantage of certain things staying fixed.
Visually, we're going to look at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:09.840
matrix multiplication.
This portion of the loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:20.240
corresponds to a matrix-vector multiplication.
We'll take a matrix and multiply it by a vector.&nbsp;

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:23.760
How do we do this?
Every column gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:27.840
multiplied by the vector and then summed.
We're going to sum along columns.&nbsp;

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:32.960
This 0 and 3 gets multiplied by the 3 and&nbsp;
7 and gets summed, and then the 1 and 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:38.880
gets multiplied by the 3 and 7 and gets summed.
There is a multiply-accumulate associated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:47.200
every single one of these entries in the matrix.
We'll draw out these four multiply-accumulates.&nbsp;

00:27:53.840 --> 00:28:01.600
Just to make sure I understand why there are&nbsp;
four multiply-accumulates: each entry in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:09.360
column that corresponds to the output&nbsp;
vector is a dot product, and in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:16.240
case it will be two multiplications and then&nbsp;
the addition of those two multiplications.&nbsp;

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:20.240
You're accumulating...
Really there's only one addition per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:23.760
dot product, but we like to start with zero.
But it includes the initialization of zero.&nbsp;

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:30.240
Yeah. We want to have quadratically more compute.&nbsp;

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:39.120
We have x times y as much&nbsp;
compute as we had before.&nbsp;

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:50.800
But we want to aim for having only&nbsp;
x times as much communication.&nbsp;

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:57.600
The intention is to get this&nbsp;
advantage term going as y.&nbsp;

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:06.080
We've laid down the multiplications.
We want to bring in a vector of size two, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:11.920
that is already in line with our columns target.
That's fine. However, we need to manage the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:16.640
communication of this matrix,&nbsp;
which exceeds our budget of x.&nbsp;

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:26.080
The idea is that in an AI context, this matrix&nbsp;
is going to stay fixed for a long period of time.&nbsp;

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:36.480
We've got some register files sitting over here.
The amount of stuff coming out of this register&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:41.920
file… this is the term that we&nbsp;
want to go as x, in some sense.&nbsp;

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:47.360
We don't want to bring this full matrix&nbsp;
in from the register file every cycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:52.320
because that would cost too much in&nbsp;
terms of wiring from the register file.&nbsp;

00:29:55.200 --> 00:30:00.720
Our key trick is that this matrix can&nbsp;
be stored locally to the systolic array.&nbsp;

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:15.280
We'll store these numbers 0, 1, 2, and 3&nbsp;
in a gate called a register that physically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:19.040
stores these numbers, and we're going to&nbsp;
reuse these numbers over and over again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:23.840
for a large number of different vectors.
The optimization here is that the nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:35.280
matrix multiplication is that you can store this&nbsp;
square quadratic thing directly where the logic is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:45.520
happening, which has an extra dimension compared&nbsp;
to the inputs that you keep swapping in and out.&nbsp;

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:46.800
That’s right.
This is the nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:52.080
what a matrix multiplication is.
You do a lot of multiplication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:55.280
to get one value out.
A dot product is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:59.760
result of a lot of multiplications.
So that optimization means you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:03.040
stuff a lot of multiplication in&nbsp;
before you get some value out of it.&nbsp;

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:07.280
That's right. Just to complete the&nbsp;
picture of concretely how that looks:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:17.440
I swapped the 3 and the 2 here.
Just like this 0 and 3 is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:25.120
to multiply by the 3 and the 7, we're going&nbsp;
to form a dot product along columns here.&nbsp;

00:31:26.480 --> 00:31:35.200
We're going to feed a 3 and a 7 in here.
This feeds into this multiplication and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:39.360
feeds into this multiplication.
Likewise, the 3 feeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:48.400
into here and also into here.
Then we're going to sum along here.&nbsp;

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:53.680
Starting at the top of a column, we feed in 0s,&nbsp;
and then coming out the bottom we get results.&nbsp;

00:31:55.840 --> 00:32:03.120
Visually, there's a dot product performed along&nbsp;
columns in a matrix, and that maps exactly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:09.040
what is done spatially in the systolic array.
This is one dot product summed vertically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:12.560
and this is a second dot&nbsp;
product also summed vertically.&nbsp;

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:17.040
What is the data that needs to go&nbsp;
into and out of the register file?&nbsp;

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:23.680
We have x amount of data coming&nbsp;
out on the output, and we also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:33.840
have x amount of data coming from the input.
With respect to the input and output vectors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:44.720
at least, we've met our goal of having only x as&nbsp;
much data going in and out of the register file.&nbsp;

00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:51.120
This leaves open the question: I said the&nbsp;
weight matrix is stored locally in the systolic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:56.800
array, so how did it get there in the first place?
At some point, you need to boot your chip and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.800 --> 00:33:02.240
populate this data, so where did that come from?
The trick is that we just do it very slowly.&nbsp;

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:05.760
We very slowly trickle-feed&nbsp;
it into the systolic array.&nbsp;

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:13.280
The simplest strategy is that we run&nbsp;
this daisy chain: feed a number in here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.280 --> 00:33:18.000
and on the next clock cycle it will move&nbsp;
down to the next entry of the systolic array.&nbsp;

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:26.320
We can do that in every column in parallel,&nbsp;
this is also going to come from here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:32.160
and that gives us another factor of&nbsp;
approximately x units of bandwidth coming in.&nbsp;

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:34.960
Would you mind repeating&nbsp;
that sentence one more time?&nbsp;

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:40.720
We know that we're going to be bringing&nbsp;
numbers only rarely into the matrix.&nbsp;

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:47.520
We just want to come up with any construction&nbsp;
at all such that the amount of wiring that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:56.720
crosses the boundary of the systolic&nbsp;
array is bounded to x and not go as xy.&nbsp;

00:33:56.720 --> 00:34:03.440
A particularly simple strategy is that&nbsp;
we bring a number into the top row of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:11.840
the systolic array in one clock cycle.
Then, for y consecutive clock cycles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:16.800
we bring in the top row every time and&nbsp;
shift all the other rows down by one.&nbsp;

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:22.080
This keeps the wiring that needs to&nbsp;
come from this expensive register file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:27.360
only down to a factor of x rather than xy.
I see. There are two questions in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:33.280
of communication: communication&nbsp;
time and communication bandwidth.&nbsp;

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:39.280
You're saying that since we're only going to be&nbsp;
loading this in once, let's minimize bandwidth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:45.760
because bandwidth equals die area.
We load it in slowly over smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:48.640
lanes because we're just going to&nbsp;
keep this value in there for a while.&nbsp;

00:34:48.640 --> 00:34:52.160
Exactly.
It's interesting to me that when we were talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.160 --> 00:34:58.240
last time about inference across many chips, the&nbsp;
big high-level thing we're trying to optimize for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.240 --> 00:35:03.760
is increasing the amount of compute per memory&nbsp;
bandwidth, that is to say, per communication.&nbsp;

00:35:03.760 --> 00:35:08.640
Here also, we're trying to increase&nbsp;
the amount of actual multiplies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:16.400
or additions relative to transporting&nbsp;
information from registers to the logic.&nbsp;

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:19.680
In both cases, you're trying to maximize&nbsp;
compute relative to communication.&nbsp;

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:27.840
This shows up all the way up and down the stack.
This is close to the bottom, to the gates.&nbsp;

00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:34.640
There's a version that's maybe even closer&nbsp;
to the gates in the precision of the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.640 --> 00:35:39.040
format that you choose to use.
We saw that same effect.&nbsp;

00:35:39.040 --> 00:35:47.280
There's a squared versus linear term going&nbsp;
on both purely in the precision of the ALU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:55.440
but also in the size of the matrix.
This unit is the next bigger unit.&nbsp;

00:35:55.440 --> 00:36:01.840
We had the multiplication circuit, and on top&nbsp;
of that we have a pretty large systolic array.&nbsp;

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:11.040
I drew it as 2x2, but older TPUs were described&nbsp;
as 128x128 of this circuit shown here.&nbsp;

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:20.400
This ends up being the most efficient known&nbsp;
circuit for implementing a matrix multiply.&nbsp;

00:36:21.360 --> 00:36:26.080
We've talked about how it seems&nbsp;
obvious that you should try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:31.120
maximize compute relative to communication.
What are non-obvious trade-offs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:35.040 --> 00:36:39.520
keep you up at night, about whether you should do&nbsp;
X or Y and it's not obvious what the answer is?&nbsp;

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:46.800
Most of the decisions in chip&nbsp;
design are sizing decisions.&nbsp;

00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:56.560
Already in what we've drawn so far… AI&nbsp;
chips all have this circuit in them.&nbsp;

00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:02.240
They have a systolic array, and somewhere near&nbsp;
it a register file providing inputs and outputs.&nbsp;

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:11.120
Even within this scope, the sizing questions you&nbsp;
have are: how big should I make my systolic array,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:23.200
and how big should I make the register file?
These two questions are coupled.&nbsp;

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:32.800
One way to think of it is to set a&nbsp;
budget for what percentage of chip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:36.960
area you want to spend on data movement.
Maybe I say that I want this to be 10%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:42.640
and the systolic array to be 90%.
Then I can size my register file.&nbsp;

00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:47.520
Bigger register files are more flexible.
They allow me to get more application-level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:54.160
performance out, but they take away from&nbsp;
the area spent on the systolic array.&nbsp;

