What determines what that is?&nbsp;

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:12.480
And what is a clock cycle of a chip?
At baseline, it's worth observing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:16.880
that chips are incredibly parallel.
You've got 100 billion transistors in a chip.&nbsp;

00:39:16.880 --> 00:39:21.200
A key thing you need to do whenever you&nbsp;
have massive parallelism is synchronize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:27.360
between the different parallel units.
In software, typically you have these very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:31.520
expensive synchronization methods like a mutex.
One thread will finish what it's doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:38.640
grab a lock stored somewhere in memory,&nbsp;
and notify the other thread that it's done.&nbsp;

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:50.080
On chips, we take a very different approach.
Every nanosecond or so, all circuitry in the chip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:57.360
will pause for a moment and synchronize.
That is the clock cycle.&nbsp;

00:39:58.240 --> 00:40:06.000
The entire chip typically goes in lockstep&nbsp;
to the next operation in one fell swoop.&nbsp;

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:17.840
What this looks like in circuitry is that&nbsp;
the clock is mediated by registers, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:17.840 --> 00:40:23.840
are these storage devices we've drawn elsewhere.
The way to think of it is: I have some storage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:29.280
holding a bit, which might be 0 or 1.
Then I have some cloud of logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:29.280 --> 00:40:42.480
which maybe is this systolic array or multiplier.
I have a bunch of inputs feeding into this cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:48.640
of logic, and eventually there's going to&nbsp;
be some output register that it writes to.&nbsp;

00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:55.040
There is a global clock signal&nbsp;
driving all these registers.&nbsp;

00:40:57.120 --> 00:41:05.120
At a certain instance in time, when the clock&nbsp;
strikes, whatever value happens to be on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:15.280
wire at that instant is what gets stored.
The challenge is that I would like to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:19.680
my clock speed run as fast as possible.
If I run at two gigahertz, I get twice as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.680 --> 00:41:23.760
many operations done per second&nbsp;
as if I run at one gigahertz.&nbsp;

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:30.400
But what that ends up meaning is that I'm very&nbsp;
sensitive to the delay through this cloud of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:37.280
logic, because any computation happening in there&nbsp;
needs to finish before the next clock cycle hits.&nbsp;

00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:53.200
A major point of optimization on any chip&nbsp;
is to make this delay as short as possible.&nbsp;

00:41:53.200 --> 00:42:02.800
Interesting. The constraint here seems&nbsp;
to be that if you add too much logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:07.760
you might risk missing the clock cycle.
But if you don't add enough, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.760 --> 00:42:15.840
leaving potential compute on the table.
Is there ever a situation where you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.840 --> 00:42:23.440
a probabilistic chance that a computation&nbsp;
finishes, or is it strictly that it either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:28.720
finishes by the clock cycle or it doesn't?
In standard chip design, you margin it such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:34.640
that there is a probability, but&nbsp;
it's many standard deviations out.&nbsp;

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:38.880
For all intents and purposes, it is a&nbsp;
reliable part and will always meet the clock.&nbsp;

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:45.760
There are some weird exceptions, like clock domain&nbsp;
crossings where you go from one clock to another.&nbsp;

00:42:45.760 --> 00:42:47.600
Then you actually do have to&nbsp;
reason about this probability.&nbsp;

00:42:47.600 --> 00:42:55.760
But in the main path, you margin it such that&nbsp;
you'll get there 25% of the clock cycle in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:55.760 --> 00:43:07.520
advance, making it very unlikely that it misses.
Where the clocks synchronize, where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:07.520 --> 00:43:11.360
registers are, is this something&nbsp;
you determine as a chip designer?&nbsp;

00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:18.960
Or is it an artifact where you want a certain&nbsp;
sequence of logic, and the software you use to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:25.120
convert your Verilog into what you send to&nbsp;
TSMC just determines that to make it work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:33.440
you have to put a register here, here, and&nbsp;
here, making sure no single step makes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.440 --> 00:43:38.240
whole chip's clock cycle longer than it has to be?
Inserting them is actually a huge part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:42.560
work of designing a chip.
It's done by a combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:42.560 --> 00:43:48.960
of manual and automatic methods.
To show the very dumb version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:48.960 --> 00:43:52.080
what you can do here, you can take&nbsp;
this logic and split it in half.&nbsp;

00:43:53.680 --> 00:44:02.400
Instead of just one cloud of logic, I can have two&nbsp;
smaller clouds of logic that do the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:13.120
but split them up by a register.
If you split it in the middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:13.120 --> 00:44:17.120
you can hit twice the clock frequency.
That's great, you get twice the performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:22.080
but at the cost of an extra&nbsp;
register, which means more storage.&nbsp;

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:24.560
Stepping back, why do we need&nbsp;
to synchronize the whole chip?&nbsp;

00:44:25.360 --> 00:44:28.949
If you imagine playing Factorio or&nbsp;
something, there's no global clock cycle.&nbsp;

00:44:28.949 --> 00:44:31.760
Things are just done when they're done.
There's iron on the plate, and you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.760 --> 00:44:36.640
take it if you want.
Taking that analogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:36.640 --> 00:44:41.840
the thing you need to be mindful of is if I&nbsp;
have two different paths through some logic.&nbsp;

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:47.520
Say I have to do computation&nbsp;
f here and computation g here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:58.400
and they're going to meet for computation h.
There's going to be manufacturing variance.&nbsp;

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:03.920
In some chips f will take a little longer;&nbsp;
in some chips g will take a little longer.&nbsp;

00:45:03.920 --> 00:45:10.640
If I have a signal propagating through, and&nbsp;
the results from f and g have to meet up at h,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:16.640
what can go wrong is that f gets&nbsp;
there early and it meets the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:16.640 --> 00:45:20.640
value of g, or the next value of g.
Ah. And h needs to know when to start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.440 --> 00:45:24.320
when the next iteration has…
Exactly.&nbsp;

00:45:24.320 --> 00:45:30.240
This explains why different chips made&nbsp;
at the same process node, the same TSMC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:32.080 --> 00:45:38.480
technology, can have different clock cycles.
Two chips made at 3 nm might have different clock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.480 --> 00:45:47.520
cycles based on whether they were able to optimize&nbsp;
to ensure no single critical path is so long that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:48.800 --> 00:45:53.600
it slows down the whole chip's clock cycle.
That's right. This optimization I showed here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.600 --> 00:45:59.040
is called pipeline register insertion.
We've inserted a register in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:59.040 --> 00:46:02.400
middle of the pipeline.
This is a pure trade-off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:08.000
between clock speed and area.
That is the easy case.&nbsp;

00:46:08.000 --> 00:46:14.320
There is a harder case too.
I drew out a pipeline of logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.320 --> 00:46:21.360
but in other cases you may have some calculation&nbsp;
which actually feeds back in on itself.&nbsp;

00:46:22.240 --> 00:46:26.320
It runs some function f and&nbsp;
then writes back to itself.&nbsp;

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:42.400
For example, this might be an addition where&nbsp;
you're adding a number every clock cycle.&nbsp;

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:49.920
This little circuit essentially sums all the&nbsp;
numbers presented on different clock cycles.&nbsp;

00:46:49.920 --> 00:46:54.320
The challenge is, if this plus&nbsp;
takes too long, what can I do?&nbsp;

00:46:54.320 --> 00:47:02.160
If I try to put a pipeline register&nbsp;
right in the middle of it, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:08.800
changes the computation that's done.
Instead of forming a running sum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.800 --> 00:47:12.880
of everything that comes in, I will&nbsp;
actually have two different running sums.&nbsp;

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:17.760
I'll end up with a running sum of the even&nbsp;
numbers and a running sum of the odd numbers.&nbsp;

00:47:18.640 --> 00:47:25.760
This constraint—where I have a loop in my logic,&nbsp;
which all chips have somewhere—is the hardest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:25.760 --> 00:47:29.280
thing to address and sets the clock cycle.
I don't understand why it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:30.960
a problem to have that.
I'm not even sure what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:36.000
would mean to have a register there.
Is it a sort of atomic operation?&nbsp;

00:47:36.560 --> 00:47:41.040
Well, plus is not really atomic.
As you just demonstrated.&nbsp;

00:47:41.040 --> 00:47:45.200
It took a whole lot of work to do a summation.
You can take the early parts of that work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.840 --> 00:47:48.320
stick a register in the middle, and&nbsp;
then take the late parts of that work.&nbsp;

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:57.040
Okay. TSMC offers a PDK which&nbsp;
specifies the primitives of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.400 --> 00:48:03.920
logic they can grant you in the chip.
It's up to them to determine that no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:10.240
primitive is bigger than the clock cycle&nbsp;
they're hoping a process node targets.&nbsp;

00:48:10.240 --> 00:48:19.280
But other than that, can't you just take&nbsp;
all the primitives from TSMC and keep adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.280 --> 00:48:24.160
registers between them as much as needed&nbsp;
until you get to your desired clock cycle?&nbsp;

00:48:24.160 --> 00:48:27.520
As a logic designer, the chip&nbsp;
architect sets the clock cycle.&nbsp;

00:48:28.240 --> 00:48:35.600
For example, the primitives you get from TSMC&nbsp;
are on the order of AND gates or full adders.&nbsp;

00:48:36.800 --> 00:48:46.720
It depends a lot on voltage and which library you&nbsp;
choose, but generally you can have about 10, 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:46.720 --> 00:48:51.760
or 30 of these sequentially in a clock cycle.
These primitives are very fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:51.760 --> 00:48:57.840
maybe 10 picoseconds.
As a logic designer, in principle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.840 --> 00:49:07.200
if you just had a register and an AND gate in a&nbsp;
loop, you could get an insanely fast clock speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:07.200 --> 00:49:16.720
more than four, five, or six gigahertz.
But if you take this really simple circuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:23.920
and look at the area you're spending&nbsp;
here… This is called one gate equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:23.920 --> 00:49:28.400
in size, so unit of one in area.
This thing is maybe a unit of eight in area.&nbsp;

00:49:31.520 --> 00:49:34.400
Again, almost all your cost&nbsp;
becomes synchronization or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:40.800
communication cost compared to the actual logic.
This would be a case where you've gone too far.&nbsp;

00:49:40.800 --> 00:49:43.840
You've made your clock speed really&nbsp;
fast at the cost of spending almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:50.480
all of your area on pipeline registers.
Interesting. So you're hinting at a dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:50.480 --> 00:49:54.720
where you can have a really fast clock speed&nbsp;
but you're not getting that much work done.&nbsp;

00:49:56.480 --> 00:50:04.560
You can have low latency but low throughput.
It hurts your throughput, in fact, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.760 --> 00:50:11.680
the throughput of your chip is the product of&nbsp;
how much you get done per clock cycle—which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:15.440
is based on area efficiency—times&nbsp;
how many clocks you get per second.&nbsp;

00:50:15.440 --> 00:50:20.720
This is actually so similar to the thing we&nbsp;
were discussing last time about batch size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:21.680 --> 00:50:28.960
where if you have a low batch size, any one&nbsp;
user can receive their next token really fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.960 --> 00:50:34.800
but the total number of tokens processed in, say,&nbsp;
an hour will be lower than it could otherwise be.&nbsp;

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:39.280
Exactly. You get less parallelism out if&nbsp;
you drive your clock speed up really high.&nbsp;

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:42.640
Language models are starting to compete&nbsp;
against the best human forecasters.&nbsp;

00:50:42.640 --> 00:50:48.240
I sat down with two senior Jane Streeters, Ron&nbsp;
Minsky and Dan Pontecorvo, and asked: at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:48.240 --> 00:50:52.160
point, does AI just do what Jane Street does?
There's a world that we should take seriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:52.160 --> 00:50:56.720
where, you know, we're going to build large&nbsp;
language models or some other AI systems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.720 --> 00:51:02.240
are like strictly smarter than all humans on the&nbsp;
planet and more capable at all cognitive tasks.&nbsp;

00:51:02.240 --> 00:51:08.160
Trading in particular feels to me as like kind&nbsp;
of AGI complete, sort of like NP complete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.160 --> 00:51:15.120
because at the end of the day trading involves&nbsp;
figuring out what things are worth, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.120 --> 00:51:18.480
making predictions about the future.
Jane Street isn't betting against AI.&nbsp;

00:51:18.480 --> 00:51:20.560
They just signed a six&nbsp;
billion dollar compute deal.&nbsp;

00:51:20.560 --> 00:51:25.360
But Ron's view is that the edge keeps moving.
I have never been more desperate to hire more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:25.360 --> 00:51:29.040
engineers and more traders than I am today.
You know, you have the usual thing of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.040 --> 00:51:31.440
the other hard parts that we&nbsp;
don't yet know how to automate.&nbsp;

00:51:31.440 --> 00:51:34.160
Well, that ends up being where&nbsp;
the competitive edge lies.&nbsp;

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:41.360
You can find these open positions and watch&nbsp;
the full interview at janestreet.com/dwarkesh.&nbsp;

00:51:41.360 --> 00:51:48.400
I remember talking to an FPGA engineer at&nbsp;
Jane Street, Clark, who helped me prep for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:48.400 --> 00:51:52.640
the previous interview we did together.
He was explaining why they use FPGAs.&nbsp;

00:51:55.200 --> 00:52:01.280
I imagine that for high-frequency trading,&nbsp;
throughput is less important than latency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.280 --> 00:52:03.360
so having very specific control over the clock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:06.480
cycle in a deterministic way&nbsp;
is the most important thing.&nbsp;

00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:14.240
Maybe it'd be interesting to talk about why&nbsp;
you can't just achieve that with an ASIC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.240 --> 00:52:23.680
or why you might use an FPGA to have deterministic&nbsp;
clock cycles for high-frequency trading.&nbsp;

00:52:25.920 --> 00:52:28.960
Let's consider the business&nbsp;
case for an FPGA versus an ASIC.&nbsp;

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:36.960
FPGAs and ASICs use largely&nbsp;
the same conceptual model.&nbsp;

00:52:36.960 --> 00:52:48.000
You have a series of gates built from small&nbsp;
primitives—ANDs, ORs, XORs—connected together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:53.120
with wires running in a fixed clock cycle.
Anything you can express in an FPGA you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:53.120 --> 00:52:55.600
can express in an ASIC too.
It will be about an order of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:55.600 --> 00:53:01.120
magnitude cheaper and have better energy&nbsp;
efficiency on an ASIC than an FPGA.&nbsp;

00:53:01.760 --> 00:53:08.640
The trade-off is that the first FPGA costs you&nbsp;
$10,000, whereas the first ASIC you make costs $30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:16.240
million because it requires an entire tape-out.
The business use case for an FPGA is when you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:16.240 --> 00:53:21.760
something that has very deterministic&nbsp;
latency, fast runtime, and high parallelism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:29.760
but you are going to change the&nbsp;
workload frequently, maybe every month.&nbsp;

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:34.240
You don't want to pay that&nbsp;
tape-out cost every time.&nbsp;

00:53:34.240 --> 00:53:42.320
How does an FPGA actually emulate the ASIC&nbsp;
programming model in a fixed piece of hardware?&nbsp;

00:53:42.320 --> 00:53:54.480
At its core, it has the two&nbsp;
components we just talked about.&nbsp;

00:53:54.480 --> 00:54:03.200
It has registers as storage&nbsp;
devices, and it has lookup tables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:03.200 --> 00:54:11.200
(LUTs) which provide all of the gates.
Then there's a third component.&nbsp;

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:30.880
We have a swarm of these registers and LUTs,&nbsp;
and they are connected by a big set of muxes.&nbsp;

00:54:30.880 --> 00:54:39.520
In front of every single one of these, we have a&nbsp;
mux which selects an input from everywhere else.&nbsp;

00:54:44.240 --> 00:54:51.040
We have a whole bunch of different&nbsp;
options feeding into all of these things.&nbsp;

00:54:54.320 --> 00:55:01.200
What this allows is essentially that when&nbsp;
I program my FPGA, I can take all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:10.080
components and superimpose a particular wiring&nbsp;
which goes through this LUT, feed it into another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:10.080 --> 00:55:14.720
LUT, send it to this register, and then feed&nbsp;
it into another LUT, or something like that.&nbsp;

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:22.560
What I've drawn in orange is how you…&nbsp;
FPGA means Field-Programmable Gate Array.&nbsp;

00:55:22.560 --> 00:55:26.080
The orange is what has been programmed in&nbsp;
the field, whereas the white is all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:26.080 --> 00:55:30.560
wires that must exist in the FPGA in order to&nbsp;
actually make the device in the first place.&nbsp;

00:55:30.560 --> 00:55:34.800
What does it mean to be programmed in the field?
Programmed in the field means the device is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:34.800 --> 00:55:36.560
being deployed in a data center.
It's sitting out in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:36.560 --> 00:55:39.680
and then you can come and program it.
Ah, not field as in like electric field.&nbsp;

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:48.800
Field as in like out there in the world, ok.
If I look at how the field programming comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:48.800 --> 00:55:56.960
out of the first lookup table and goes&nbsp;
into a second one, how does that work?&nbsp;

00:55:57.680 --> 00:56:03.040
Where are the wires that make that happen?
I got a little bit lazy in drawing all of these.&nbsp;

00:56:03.040 --> 00:56:11.120
Every single device here has a mux sitting in&nbsp;
front of it, which can select from all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.120 --> 00:56:19.760
nearby circuits that are available.
The actual configuration of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:19.760 --> 00:56:28.240
FPGA amounts to the mux control.
In this mux, we have the data inputs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:28.240 --> 00:56:34.320
and we have the control that selects.
There's a little storage device sitting next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:34.320 --> 00:56:42.080
to every single one of these muxes saying, "This&nbsp;
is where you're going to source your input from."&nbsp;

00:56:43.920 --> 00:56:47.600
Programming it consists of configuring&nbsp;
every single one of these muxes.&nbsp;

00:56:47.600 --> 00:56:49.840
That makes sense. What is happening&nbsp;
inside of the lookup table?&nbsp;

00:56:51.840 --> 00:56:56.160
The lookup table is also going to have a&nbsp;
little bit of control telling it what to do.&nbsp;

00:56:56.160 --> 00:57:03.360
Its purpose is to configurably&nbsp;
take the role of an AND gate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.360 --> 00:57:09.520
OR gate, XOR, or any of those different things.
There are many ways you could consider doing that.&nbsp;

00:57:10.880 --> 00:57:25.920
The way it's done in traditional&nbsp;
FPGAs… A lookup table has four bits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:25.920 --> 00:57:31.440
of input and one bit of output.
How many different functions are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:34.880
there from four bits to one bit?
There are 16 different functions.&nbsp;

00:57:37.200 --> 00:57:49.280
You can tabulate this as 16 different numbers.
You've got a table of 0111001, 16 entries.&nbsp;

00:57:52.080 --> 00:57:55.520
This table is stored in&nbsp;
this blue configuration bit.&nbsp;

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:08.080
It views these four bits as binary, looks up the&nbsp;
relevant row of the table, and emits that bit.&nbsp;

00:58:08.080 --> 00:58:10.640
This is essentially a truth-table&nbsp;
view of lookup tables.&nbsp;

00:58:11.360 --> 00:58:18.640
Okay, so if you think about an AND gate, OR gate,&nbsp;
NOR gate, XOR gate, these all take as input…&nbsp;

00:58:18.640 --> 00:58:25.200
Those are two-input functions. Sometimes we have&nbsp;
a three-input function, like a three-way XOR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:25.200 --> 00:58:27.440
or a four-way XOR.
In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:32.880
does it just depend on how big it is?
The typical size for LUTs is four inputs.&nbsp;

00:58:32.880 --> 00:58:37.840
It's sort of just a sweet spot.
There’s another compute vs. communication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:37.840 --> 00:58:39.840
trade-off here.
If it has too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:39.840 --> 00:58:45.760
few inputs, you need to use more LUTs.
Basically the lookup table is a truth table.&nbsp;

00:58:47.440 --> 00:58:49.520
With a truth table, you can&nbsp;
program in any gate you want.&nbsp;

00:58:50.800 --> 00:58:53.920
So instead of a lookup table, you can&nbsp;
just think of it as a programmable gate.&nbsp;

00:58:53.920 --> 00:58:59.520
That's right. One of the things you can do&nbsp;
here is you can see where the rule of thumb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:59.520 --> 00:59:04.000
that an FPGA is an order of magnitude&nbsp;
more expensive than an ASIC comes from.&nbsp;

00:59:04.000 --> 00:59:07.440
You count how many gates would&nbsp;
be inside this lookup table.&nbsp;

00:59:10.240 --> 00:59:13.600
We can view this lookup table&nbsp;
essentially as one of these muxes.&nbsp;

00:59:14.240 --> 00:59:18.800
It has to select between 16 different values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:37.600
so it's a mux with n=16 options and p=1 bits.
As we saw earlier, this circuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:37.600 --> 00:59:45.360
costs n times p many gates.
So it costs np, which is 16,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:45.360 --> 00:59:52.080
AND gates, and also 16 ORs.
This circuit being the mux?&nbsp;

00:59:52.080 --> 00:59:55.520
Exactly, the mux.
The mux that goes into the lookup table?&nbsp;

00:59:56.240 --> 01:00:02.240
The lookup table itself you can think of&nbsp;
as being a big mux that selects from all 16&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:02.240 --> 01:00:05.520
rows down to one output.
That's the lookup table.&nbsp;

01:00:05.520 --> 01:00:08.720
But the way you've drawn it here,&nbsp;
there's a mux and then a lookup table.&nbsp;

01:00:08.720 --> 01:00:12.720
It's muxes all the way down.
There is a second mux that is inside here.&nbsp;

01:00:12.720 --> 01:00:18.880
This mux is this mux.
And the other mux is just saying…&nbsp;

01:00:18.880 --> 01:00:23.600
where it came from in this mess of gates.
Right, and the second mux is, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:23.600 --> 01:00:29.840
now you have one value, but that&nbsp;
value is still a four-bit value."&nbsp;

01:00:29.840 --> 01:00:35.440
Yeah, I've selected four bits from the soup.
Then I use those four bits to select which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:35.440 --> 01:00:45.200
entry in the lookup table I'm going to use.
Suppose in the first mux you're pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.200 --> 01:00:52.080
from eight nearby registers as input.
That's a total of 32 bits going in.&nbsp;

01:00:53.440 --> 01:00:58.640
Out of that, four bits come out.
Those four bits go into the second mux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:58.640 --> 01:01:02.720
which is inside the lookup table.
In this case, these registers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:02.720 --> 01:01:06.400
are single-bit registers.
If there are eight nearby&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:06.400 --> 01:01:11.440
registers and lookup tables, then I&nbsp;
have eight bits total coming in nearby.&nbsp;

01:01:11.440 --> 01:01:21.760
I select from eight down to four different values.
There are actually four different muxes, a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:26.400
mux associated with each of these input bits.
Each of them is selecting one out of eight.&nbsp;

01:01:26.400 --> 01:01:30.400
Where are those eight coming from?
Nearby registers and other LUTs.&nbsp;

01:01:30.400 --> 01:01:32.320
Each register is one bit.
Yes.&nbsp;

01:01:33.360 --> 01:01:40.720
I guess AMD or whoever makes these FPGAs&nbsp;
still has to be opinionated about which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:47.680
registers are connected to which registers.
You can program in the actual gates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:47.680 --> 01:01:52.080
but they add a wire in the connect…&nbsp;
the communication topology, right?&nbsp;

01:01:53.760 --> 01:02:00.000
You get flexibility at a local grain.
There's a nearby neighborhood you can select from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:06.160
but for more coarse, long-distance&nbsp;
connections, they form an opinion on that.&nbsp;

01:02:06.160 --> 01:02:11.360
And the reason it's 10x slower is why?
If you look at the cost of building this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:11.360 --> 01:02:17.120
lookup table, it's 32 gates.
It can give me the equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:17.120 --> 01:02:21.280
of—what’s an interesting thing I&nbsp;
can do here—a four-way AND gate.&nbsp;

01:02:26.960 --> 01:02:35.440
A four-way AND means AND, AND,&nbsp;
and then an AND of an AND.&nbsp;

01:02:36.400 --> 01:02:40.720
This is a circuit I could implement in&nbsp;
an ASIC directly using three AND gates.&nbsp;

01:02:41.520 --> 01:02:46.640
Using a LUT, I can also implement it, but&nbsp;
it's going to take 32 gates instead of three.&nbsp;

01:02:47.200 --> 01:02:58.560
So the overhead is really coming from&nbsp;
the fact that there's a more concise&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:58.560 --> 01:03:05.440
way to describe a truth table than listing out&nbsp;
every single possible combination of inputs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:06.400 --> 01:03:11.360
which is just to write out the gate.
Yes, to place down the polysilicon&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:11.360 --> 01:03:15.120
and the wires and so on.
Interesting. One important point you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:15.120 --> 01:03:21.600
made to me is that the reason they prefer FPGAs to&nbsp;
CPUs is that they get deterministic clock cycles.&nbsp;

01:03:21.600 --> 01:03:28.720
They know when a packet will come in and go out.
Why isn't that a guarantee in CPUs?&nbsp;

01:03:28.720 --> 01:03:32.400
You can actually design a CPU that&nbsp;
has deterministic latency as well.&nbsp;

01:03:34.640 --> 01:03:40.480
In fact, the processors inside a lot of&nbsp;
AI chips also have deterministic latency.&nbsp;

01:03:40.480 --> 01:03:43.120
Groq has advertised this. TPUs&nbsp;
have that in the core as well.&nbsp;

01:03:46.080 --> 01:03:50.400
The challenge is getting deterministic&nbsp;
latency and high speed at the same time.&nbsp;

01:03:53.280 --> 01:04:01.520
Non-deterministic latency comes from&nbsp;
specific design choices in a CPU.&nbsp;

01:04:03.520 --> 01:04:08.640
It's actually possible to remove those design&nbsp;
choices and make a CPU with deterministic latency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:08.640 --> 01:04:13.760
but those are not very attractive in the&nbsp;
market, so people don't make those CPUs anymore.&nbsp;

01:04:16.960 --> 01:04:24.960
In some sense, deterministic latency is a simpler&nbsp;
starting point, and some chip designers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:24.960 --> 01:04:33.440
added things in to make it non-deterministic.
To take a concrete example, probably the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:33.440 --> 01:04:39.280
important source of non-determinism&nbsp;
on a CPU is the CPU cache itself.&nbsp;

01:04:43.920 --> 01:04:50.640
In a CPU, you have the CPU die itself,&nbsp;
and then DDR memory off on the side.&nbsp;

01:04:54.400 --> 01:05:07.120
You have a cache system inside that remembers&nbsp;
recent accesses to DDR and stores them.&nbsp;

01:05:09.200 --> 01:05:11.600
When I'm running through my&nbsp;
CPU instructions, every time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:12.240 --> 01:05:17.600
I have an instruction that accesses memory, it&nbsp;
first checks if the data was stored in the cache.&nbsp;

01:05:17.600 --> 01:05:23.840
If not, it fetches it from DDR.
This is a huge optimization.&nbsp;

01:05:23.840 --> 01:05:27.200
The cache is two orders of&nbsp;
magnitude faster than the DDR.&nbsp;

01:05:27.200 --> 01:05:33.600
If you never used the cache, basically all&nbsp;
programs would run a hundred times slower.&nbsp;

01:05:34.160 --> 01:05:38.240
The presence of a cache is absolutely necessary&nbsp;
for a CPU to run at a reasonable speed.&nbsp;

01:05:39.360 --> 01:05:45.760
But whether or not you get a cache hit&nbsp;
depends on the ambient environment of the CPU:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:45.760 --> 01:05:48.880
what other programs are running, what has&nbsp;
run recently, and what the random number&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.880 --> 01:05:53.200
generator inside the cache system is doing.
That is a big source of non-determinism&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:53.200 --> 01:05:58.800
in the runtime of a CPU.
That is the memory system for a CPU.&nbsp;

01:06:01.600 --> 01:06:06.320
The big thing you can do differently&nbsp;
is, instead of having the hardware say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:06.320 --> 01:06:10.720
"I’m going to read memory" and then the&nbsp;
hardware decides whether or not it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:11.280 --> 01:06:18.560
from the cache, you can bake this decision&nbsp;
into software, a different design philosophy.&nbsp;

01:06:22.480 --> 01:06:28.640
You see this in TPUs, for example.
I'll draw the same diagram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:28.640 --> 01:06:36.240
but I'll call it a scratchpad.
The main difference is… This would be a TPU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:36.240 --> 01:06:41.680
and you have HBM in this case rather than&nbsp;
DDR, but it's still an off-chip memory.&nbsp;

01:06:43.280 --> 01:06:48.480
Instead of the software saying "first access&nbsp;
memory" and letting the hardware decide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:48.480 --> 01:06:53.280
you have one kind of instruction that goes to&nbsp;
the scratchpad and a totally different kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:53.280 --> 01:06:58.160
of instruction that goes to HBM.
This style is generically known&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:58.160 --> 01:07:04.160
as scratchpad instead of cache.
The key distinction is that you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:04.160 --> 01:07:08.480
one kind of instruction that says "read or write&nbsp;
scratchpad," and a totally different instruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:08.480 --> 01:07:12.880
that says "read or write HBM."
So scratchpad being the cache.&nbsp;

01:07:12.880 --> 01:07:20.000
Yeah, this thing here is the scratchpad.
Stepping way back: people say computers have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.320 --> 01:07:28.240
"von Neumann architecture", where there's&nbsp;
this serial processing of information.&nbsp;

01:07:28.240 --> 01:07:30.400
Maybe it's just because we've&nbsp;
been talking about parallel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:30.400 --> 01:07:40.720
accelerators, but the FPGA is super parallel.
The AI accelerators, the TPUs, are super parallel.&nbsp;

01:07:41.280 --> 01:07:44.960
Even CPUs are super parallel if you&nbsp;
think about all the cores they have.&nbsp;

01:07:46.800 --> 01:07:50.080
In what sense is modern hardware&nbsp;
actually the von Neumann architecture?&nbsp;

01:07:50.080 --> 01:07:52.560
Is that actually a fair way&nbsp;
to describe modern hardware?&nbsp;

01:07:52.560 --> 01:07:58.480
I think it's a fair way to describe CPUs.
The amount of parallelism you get on a CPU is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:58.480 --> 01:08:05.680
about 100 cores times maybe 16-way vector&nbsp;
units, so about 1,000-way parallelism on a CPU.&nbsp;

01:08:06.800 --> 01:08:15.280
One question: there is a die being used for&nbsp;
the CPU, and if there are fewer threads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:15.280 --> 01:08:22.400
just as a matter of transistor voltages&nbsp;
switching on and off, is it that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:22.400 --> 01:08:26.240
literally one control flow—a small part of the&nbsp;
die—where voltages are switching on and off?&nbsp;

01:08:28.240 --> 01:08:35.600
How do you actually occupy the die area of a CPU…
If there are so few cores, what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:35.600 --> 01:08:37.360
you spending all of the die on?
Yeah, what is happening there?&nbsp;

01:08:37.360 --> 01:08:39.840
The cores are just much&nbsp;
bigger and more complicated.&nbsp;

01:08:44.240 --> 01:08:50.640
We should compare a CPU core, which takes&nbsp;
up one one-hundredth of the die, to a LUT.&nbsp;

01:08:50.640 --> 01:08:54.720
A LUT is only 16 gates.
It's clear why there are so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:54.720 --> 01:09:04.320
more LUTs in an FPGA than cores in a CPU.
But why are there more CUDA cores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:04.320 --> 01:09:10.000
for example, than CPU cores?
What's the difference between a CPU and a GPU?&nbsp;

01:09:13.600 --> 01:09:22.720
Inside the CPU, one big use&nbsp;
of the area is the cache.&nbsp;

01:09:23.760 --> 01:09:28.240
Very little is actually the ALUs.
Mostly it's these register&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:28.240 --> 01:09:36.320
files rather than the logic units.
Both of those have equivalents in a GPU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:36.320 --> 01:09:39.040
so that's not a big difference.
But the thing that does not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:39.040 --> 01:09:47.200
an equivalent in a GPU is the branch predictor.
There is a whole big area in the CPU which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:49.120 --> 01:09:58.320
just a bunch of predictors saying when the next&nbsp;
branch will be and where the branch target is.&nbsp;

01:09:58.320 --> 01:10:03.680
Stripping a lot of that out, as well&nbsp;
as making these register files tighter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:06.400 --> 01:10:10.160
drives a lot of the GPU gains over the CPU.
What is the purpose of the branch predictor?&nbsp;

01:10:10.160 --> 01:10:13.440
To execute both branches at&nbsp;
once, or what does it do?&nbsp;

01:10:13.440 --> 01:10:22.000
The issue is that when I've got a series&nbsp;
of instructions, if I have a branch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:27.920 --> 01:10:32.880
the actual step of processing an&nbsp;
instruction takes a really long time.&nbsp;

01:10:32.880 --> 01:10:39.840
It takes maybe five nanoseconds.
The time to notice that I've got a branch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:43.760 --> 01:10:49.920
evaluate whether the Boolean is true, update&nbsp;
the program counter to the new target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:49.920 --> 01:10:56.960
and then read from the instruction memory&nbsp;
could take five nanoseconds to finish.&nbsp;

01:10:56.960 --> 01:10:59.680
So in reality, this may&nbsp;
finish somewhere down here.&nbsp;

01:11:03.600 --> 01:11:07.120
I want to run a clock speed that is much&nbsp;
faster than what five nanoseconds allows.&nbsp;

01:11:07.920 --> 01:11:12.240
Five nanoseconds is a 200 MHz clock speed.
I would like to run at one or two gigahertz.&nbsp;

01:11:13.200 --> 01:11:20.240
So I need to run other instructions&nbsp;
while the branch is being evaluated.&nbsp;

01:11:21.360 --> 01:11:26.880
I just want to keep running the&nbsp;
instructions that happen after me.&nbsp;

01:11:27.680 --> 01:11:30.480
But that might have been wrong.
If the branch ended up being taken, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:30.480 --> 01:11:34.560
I need to know that instead of evaluating these&nbsp;
instructions, I actually need to jump to wherever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:34.560 --> 01:11:42.080
the target is and run those instructions instead.
The purpose of the branch predictor is to predict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:42.080 --> 01:11:48.720
five cycles earlier, that a branch is going to&nbsp;
happen, before you even get to that instruction.&nbsp;

01:11:49.520 --> 01:11:52.480
If I think about how the brain works&nbsp;
versus what you're describing here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.480 --> 01:11:59.760
at a high level the differences might be that&nbsp;
while you can do structured sparsity in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:59.760 --> 01:12:07.840
accelerators and save yourself some area that you&nbsp;
would have otherwise had to dedicate to gates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:07.840 --> 01:12:12.800
in the brain there's unstructured sparsity.
Any neuron can connect to any other neuron, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:12.800 --> 01:12:18.800
not in ways where they have the column aligned.
Then there's the fact that memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:18.800 --> 01:12:22.000
and compute are co-located.
Although I guess you could say in a way the memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:22.000 --> 01:12:25.360
and compute are co-located on these dies too.
This is exactly the co-location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:25.360 --> 01:12:30.400
in some sense, of the memory and compute.
So maybe that isn't a big difference.&nbsp;

01:12:30.400 --> 01:12:40.720
Another big difference is that the clock cycle&nbsp;
on the brain is much slower than on computers.&nbsp;

01:12:40.720 --> 01:12:47.600
Partly that's to preserve energy, because the&nbsp;
faster the clock cycle, the bigger the voltage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:47.600 --> 01:12:54.880
needs to be in order for the signal to settle&nbsp;
and to identify what state a transistor is in.&nbsp;

01:12:54.880 --> 01:12:56.160
That’s right.
I don't know if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:56.160 --> 01:13:05.440
you have any commentary on what the brain&nbsp;
might be doing versus how these chips work.&nbsp;

01:13:06.720 --> 01:13:16.560
Let's take the clock speed one first.
The clock speed is quite high on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:16.560 --> 01:13:26.000
chip because that drives higher throughput.
When we compare a GPU running some workload,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:26.000 --> 01:13:29.440
it's running batch size 1,000.
Whereas the brain is not running&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:29.440 --> 01:13:35.760
batch size 1,000, there's only one of me.
You could imagine saying, "Take a GPU and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:35.760 --> 01:13:39.360
instead of running at a gigahertz, run&nbsp;
it at a megahertz," and that would start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:39.360 --> 01:13:45.600
to look a little more like the equivalent&nbsp;
things you're talking about in the brain.&nbsp;

01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:56.720
But in the way silicon works, that does not give&nbsp;
you a 1,000x advantage in energy efficiency.&nbsp;

01:13:58.480 --> 01:14:07.840
What it ends up looking like is you just&nbsp;
run this circuit once to stabilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:07.840 --> 01:14:10.720
and then it will sit idle&nbsp;
for a long period of time.&nbsp;

01:14:10.720 --> 01:14:12.720
It doesn't consume a lot of&nbsp;
energy while it's sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:12.720 --> 01:14:18.720
idle because most of the energy is consumed&nbsp;
in toggling bits from zero to one and back.&nbsp;

01:14:22.640 --> 01:14:26.560
Let's talk about the energy&nbsp;
consumption of a circuit like this.&nbsp;

01:14:26.560 --> 01:14:32.560
The way to think of a bit being stored is that&nbsp;
you've deposited some charge in a capacitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:32.560 --> 01:14:38.640
sitting somewhere in the chip implicitly.
It becomes charged when the bit becomes a one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.640 --> 01:14:41.920
and then it becomes discharged&nbsp;
when it next goes to a zero.&nbsp;

01:14:41.920 --> 01:14:45.120
That cycle of charging the capacitor&nbsp;
and then dumping that charge out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:45.120 --> 01:14:49.920
to ground is where the energy is consumed.
This is called the dynamic or switching power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:49.920 --> 01:14:54.160
and it's most of the energy consumption of a chip.
There is some other energy consumption just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:54.160 --> 01:15:00.880
coming from the fact that insulators&nbsp;
aren't perfect, but we'll discard that.&nbsp;

01:15:00.880 --> 01:15:07.120
Most of the energy consumption comes from&nbsp;
toggling from zero to one and back to zero.&nbsp;

01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:12.080
If you run a chip much slower and you only&nbsp;
clock it once every thousand clock cycles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:12.720 --> 01:15:16.240
you will have 1,000 times fewer transitions.
It will be about 1,000 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:16.240 --> 01:15:20.960
less energy consumption.
But it's not a substantial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:20.960 --> 01:15:25.040
advantage in energy efficiency.
Okay, so you described how a TPU&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:25.040 --> 01:15:29.040
works at a high level.
What is the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:29.040 --> 01:15:37.200
at a high level between how a GPU and a TPU work?
There is a high-level organization principle that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:37.200 --> 01:15:41.920
is different, and then inside&nbsp;
the cores things are different.&nbsp;

01:15:41.920 --> 01:15:49.680
Looking at the high level, we'll&nbsp;
take a GPU and a TPU and see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.320 --> 01:15:55.040
the top-level block structure looks like.
If you think of this as the whole chip&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:55.040 --> 01:16:03.200
in each case, the organization&nbsp;
of the GPU is mostly a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:03.200 --> 01:16:12.080
almost-identical units, which are the SMs.
They've got an L2 memory in the middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:12.080 --> 01:16:21.520
and then a bunch more of these SMs on the bottom.
So there is a fairly regular grid of cores.&nbsp;

01:16:24.000 --> 01:16:33.600
If we look at a TPU in comparison, you end&nbsp;
up with much coarser-grained units of logic.&nbsp;

01:16:34.160 --> 01:16:48.400
You end up with just a few matrix units,&nbsp;
which are the big systolic arrays.&nbsp;

01:16:48.400 --> 01:16:55.920
In the middle you've got some vector unit, and&nbsp;
then you've got your matrix units at the bottom.&nbsp;

01:17:00.640 --> 01:17:06.240
These matrix units with a vector unit in&nbsp;
the middle make up the whole TPU chip.&nbsp;

01:17:06.240 --> 01:17:10.480
You can think of scaling this thing&nbsp;
down into a really tiny unit with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:10.480 --> 01:17:15.760
smaller matrix unit and a smaller vector&nbsp;
unit, and that is sort of what an SM is.&nbsp;

01:17:16.400 --> 01:17:24.400
From a very high-level point of view, the GPU has&nbsp;
a lot of tiny TPUs tiled across the whole chip.&nbsp;

01:17:24.400 --> 01:17:31.200
Oh, interesting. You're suggesting the tensor&nbsp;
core within a streaming SM is analogous to an MXU?&nbsp;

01:17:31.200 --> 01:17:38.480
Yeah, it's all very similar.
I see. If you had more lack of structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:38.480 --> 01:17:44.080
having a bunch of tiny TPUs makes a lot of sense.
Whereas if you just have huge matrix&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:44.080 --> 01:17:54.400
multiplications, you might want to avoid the&nbsp;
cost of having individual SMs with their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.400 --> 01:17:58.400
registers and warp schedulers.
Why not just make a huge thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:58.400 --> 01:18:04.320
and amortize those costs across the whole thing?
This shows up in how large you can grow things.&nbsp;

01:18:04.320 --> 01:18:08.240
We've seen this theme, especially with the&nbsp;
systolic array, where a larger systolic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:08.240 --> 01:18:14.480
array amortizes the register file costs better.
This design allows you to have larger systolic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.480 --> 01:18:20.400
arrays, whereas the GPU design constrains&nbsp;
you to having small units of everything.&nbsp;

01:18:21.600 --> 01:18:29.520
There is a trade-off, however.
Because of this coarse-grained&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:29.520 --> 01:18:34.400
separation of things, you need to move a lot of&nbsp;
data from the vector unit to the matrix units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:37.200 --> 01:18:46.320
through just two lines of perimeter here.
If you look at the equivalent thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:46.320 --> 01:18:50.480
in a GPU, you've got vector units everywhere, and&nbsp;
you can move data through many different lines.&nbsp;

01:18:55.520 --> 01:19:00.240
The amount of data you can move between a&nbsp;
vector unit and a matrix unit is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:00.240 --> 01:19:08.960
much higher in a GPU than in a TPU.
Instead of having to move all the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:08.960 --> 01:19:16.720
through just two lines, you're moving&nbsp;
it through 16 lines of wiring in a GPU.&nbsp;

01:19:16.720 --> 01:19:21.200
Right. But also you might&nbsp;
have to move across less area.&nbsp;

01:19:21.200 --> 01:19:27.920
Which is an energy saving as well.
So if you can operate entirely within&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:27.920 --> 01:19:33.760
an SM, the data movement is much smaller.
But the moment you want to operate across SMs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:33.760 --> 01:19:37.760
it becomes more complicated and expensive.
So you don’t have to comment, but one might&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:37.760 --> 01:19:45.040
expect that a thing MatX might try to do&nbsp;
is get the GPU-like smaller structure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:45.760 --> 01:19:55.840
systolic arrays surrounded by SRAM, but at the&nbsp;
same time make it so that the things you need in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:55.840 --> 01:20:03.200
an SM to support the CUDA architecture—which&nbsp;
take a bunch of space—you might discard.&nbsp;

01:20:05.280 --> 01:20:09.440
We've talked publicly about something we&nbsp;
call a splittable systolic array, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:09.440 --> 01:20:13.600
in some sense you can think of as big systolic&nbsp;
arrays that can be small systolic arrays too.&nbsp;

01:20:13.600 --> 01:20:16.640
Cool. Okay, I think that's&nbsp;
a good note to close on.&nbsp;

01:20:16.640 --> 01:20:18.960
Reiner, thank you so much.
Thanks, Dwarkesh.

